<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurya Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mauryas, Mauryan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maurya Empire was the first state to unite most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> around 321 BCE. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> it reached its greatest extent, and Ashoka's edicts promoted dhamma and Buddhist ethics. It had a centralised administration described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> and declined after Ashoka's death, ending around 185 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurya-empire.jpg" alt="Maurya Empire" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Jonathan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurya_Empire,_c.250_BCE_network_model_v01.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Maurya Empire had its power base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>. Its origins are shrouded in legend. Greek sources refer to confrontations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, whom they call Sandrokottos, but say almost nothing of his conquest of the Nanda Empire, while Indian sources narrate only that conquest. Buddhist texts place him in the Moriya clan, and Jain texts call him the son of an imperial superintendent of peacocks.</p><p>Through military conquests and diplomatic treaties Chandragupta extended his rule as far west as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and as far south as the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> core, the technology and infrastructure of the time limited how deeply his rule could penetrate society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chandragupta was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bindusara">Bindusara</a>, and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, his grandson. Ashoka crushed revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> as a prince. His conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> in 262–261 BCE became the turning point of his life: an estimated 100,000 soldiers and civilians were killed, and many more were affected by the destruction. Ashoka felt remorse, embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and renounced war. He sent missionaries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and propagated his own dhamma in edicts, most commonly set up along well-travelled roads.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> was followed for fifty years by weaker emperors. None of his sons took the throne: Mahinda became a Buddhist monk and Kunala was blinded. Dasharatha, Ashoka's grandson, lost many territories, some later retaken by Samprati. The last Mauryan ruler, Brihadratha, was killed by his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a> during a military parade, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a> arose.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s empire consisted of five parts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, with the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, was ruled directly. Four provinces, with capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosali">Tosali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvarnagiri">Suvarnagiri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, were governed by princes. Historians now often see the empire as a network of core regions and trade routes rather than a solid territory. The major rock edicts are found in the frontier provinces and are absent in large parts of the supposed territory, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> credited the empire with an army of 600,000 infantry, 30,000 cavalry, 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and 9,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. Roads such as the winter road Uttarapath, linking eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, promoted trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins were widely used. The population is estimated at between 15 and 30 million. The empire favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Ajivikism over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurya Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They appear in the Ashokan Edicts as neighbours of the <b>Maurya Empire</b>, and the Sangam literature names early kings such as Karikala and Kocengannan, with Urayur as their oldest capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Demetrius I is generally considered the Greco-Bactrian king who first launched the Greek expansion into India, around 200 BCE, and so the founder of the Indo-Greek realm; the overthrow of the <b>Mauryas</b> by Pushyamitra Shunga about 185 BCE is thought to have encouraged it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Vajji league, Kosala and Avanti created the territorial core on which the Nanda Empire and then the <b>Maurya Empire</b> built the first state to rule most of the Indian subcontinent, and Pataliputra, founded on a fortified Magadhan village, remained the capital of the Nandas and Mauryas and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Maurya Empire</b> withdrew from the region in the second century BCE, the Satavahanas rose in the northern Deccan, in what is now Maharashtra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His eastern campaign (c. 305–303 BCE) brought him into confrontation with Chandragupta Maurya, founder of the <b>Mauryan Empire</b>, who had seized Alexander's Indian territories after the Macedonian withdrawal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…empress converted to Buddhism to justify imperial authority and the conquest of Chen; the emperor presented himself as a Cakravartin king who would defend the faith by force, and in 601 distributed relics of the Buddha to temples throughout China, an imitation of Ashoka of the <b>Maurya Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
